  "query_preview": "..Dry and mild weather continues across our area this\nafternoon, but this will soon change with a couple of significant\nweather systems due to impact our region over the next few days.\nThe first system is due after midnight tonight through Friday\nevening. A shortwave over the Pacific will move northeastward\nacross Arizona, drawing northward some deep moisture with\nprecipitable water values close to the 99th percentile for late\nOctober. This first system will also bring increased wind shear \nand instability, peaking over areas of Yavapai and Gila counties \nand perhaps up to the Mogollon Rim during the day Friday. A few\nstrong to severe thunderstorms are expected, and this area is in a\nMarginal Risk for severe weather on the latest SPC outlooks. The\nmain threats with these storms will be large hail and damaging\nwinds. A Flash Flood Watch is also in effect Friday into Friday\nevening for Yavapai and Gila counties where a general 0.50\" to\n1.00\" rainfall is forecast, locally higher amounts to 2.00\" in \nany training thunderstorms. Outside of the watch area, more\nisolated instances of flooding will still be possible, including\nthe Mogollon Rim up to the Grand Canyon/Kaibab Plateau. A lull in\nactivity is still forecast beginning later Friday evening and\ncontinuing through mid evening Saturday as we are in between\nsystems. Mid and upper levels dry out, with just enough low level\nmoisture lingering for an isolated shower, but this is looking \nminor for any Halloween related outdoor activities.\n\nLate Saturday night through Monday morning - the latest guidance\ncontinues to show a major rainfall event across much of our \nforecast area. Deep moisture from tropical origins is forecast to\nmove into our area from the southwest, with even higher\nprecipitable water values of 0.75\"-1.25\" nearing the maximum \nobserved for this time of year. Deep low pressure diving down the\nwest coast is forecast by most guidance to at least partially\nphase with a low off the Pacific, resulting in an extended period\nof large scale ascent, locally enhanced by strong southwesterly\nupslope flow. Rainfall amounts are looking impressive, with NBM\nmean values of 2-3\" widespread over the Mogollon Rim, White Mtns,\nand Gila/Yavapai counties during this period. Given the duration\nof the event and moisture anomalies, locally higher amounts are\nnot out of the question in the most favored upslope locations. NBM\n90th percentile rainfall in these areas is 4-5\" in the latest run.\nNorth/east of the Mogollon Rim the orographic effect will not be\nas significant, though some enhancement will occur along the Black\nMesa/Chuskas as well as the Kaibab Plateau. Mean rainfall in these\nareas is still 0.75\"-1.50\" with locally higher amounts. Given the\ncurrent degree of saturation of soils due to previous storm \nsystems, we have opted to include all of our forecast area in a\nFlash Flood Watch beginning at 11 PM MST Saturday and continuing \nthrough 5 AM Monday. Flash flooding and small stream flooding will\nbe the initial threat, transitioning to elevated streams and\npossibly flooding on the larger creeks/rivers even after the\nprecipitation ends. Several of the latest stream/river forecasts\nare rising to near or above flood stage by Sunday into Monday,\nincluding streams coming off the Mogollon Rim into Yavapai/Gila,\nas well as Moenkopi and the Little Colorado River. Improving\nweather conditions are forecast from west to east through the day\nMonday, with generally fair weather expected most of next week.",
